+- `'json'`: Parse the value as JSON. A value that is not valid JSON stays a string. An integer beyond `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` is returned exactly as a `BigInt` instead of being rounded, so 64-bit IDs survive.
+
+```js
+import queryString from 'query-string';
+
+queryString.parse('filter=%7B%22accountId%22%3A1274593846275813376%7D&bad=%7B', {
+	types: {
+		filter: 'json',
+		bad: 'json',
+	},
+});
+//=> {bad: '{', filter: {accountId: 1274593846275813376n}}
+```
+
+Note: With an `arrayFormat` like `'comma'`, the value is split before it is parsed, so JSON that contains the separator is split too. Use the default `arrayFormat: 'none'` for JSON values.
+
